# Blocky Single-Round Match and Exit Controls Plan

## Current Architecture

- Blocky is a browser-only React + TypeScript game built with Vite and Tailwind. Match state is in-memory and reducer-owned; these issues require no server, authentication, storage, database, or schema changes.
- `src/App.tsx` owns `useReducer(gameReducer, ...)`, passes the canonical `MatchState` to `ScreenRouter`, and renders the live `MatchProgressPanel` beside every active screen.
- `src/state/gameState.ts` is the single match-flow authority. It owns active player, active round, pause/restart session state, selected difficulty, recorded times, handoff, results, and winner calculation. `ROUND_COUNT` in `src/types/game.ts` drives all completion checks.
- `src/components/screens/ScreenRouter.tsx` composes the gameplay hooks, playfield, timer, HUD, and screen actions. The start, difficulty, round-end, handoff, and results views are separate components under `src/components/screens/`.
- Reducer coverage is in `src/state/gameState.test.ts`; complete two-player journeys are exercised by `src/state/fullPlaythrough.e2e.test.ts`.

## Planned Module Structure

- `src/types/game.ts`: change the canonical `ROUND_COUNT` from three to one. Keep `RoundTime` and `activeRound` structurally intact so display and state contracts remain stable; every player simply records one entry with `round: 1`.
- `src/state/gameState.ts`: retain the existing reducer API and use the updated round count to transition directly from a completed player round to the player handoff or final results. Preserve pause/restart semantics and score calculation using the one recorded survival time per player.
- `src/components/screens/` and `src/components/game/`: update visible instructions, HUD, scoreboard, round-end, handoff, and results labels to describe one turn per player rather than three rounds or a best round. Use the shared `ROUND_COUNT` for numeric progress where a count remains useful.
- `src/components/screens/ScreenRouter.tsx`: add reducer-dispatched exit callbacks to the in-play and round-end views. The exit action returns the session to the start screen by creating a clean match state; it must not leave an active timer, delayed game-over completion, or stale match progress visible.
- `src/state/gameState.test.ts`: extend reducer tests for direct single-round transitions and the exit action from valid and invalid screens.
- `src/state/fullPlaythrough.e2e.test.ts`: adapt the full journey to one completed turn per player and assert correct winner/tie behavior. Add an exit journey test that confirms a fresh start state and no residual player data.

## Issue Dependency Map

1. **#53 — Single round per player:** change the canonical count and reducer transitions first. This establishes the behavior every later UI and test update consumes.
2. **#54 — Single-round wording:** depends on #53 so all player-facing copy reflects the actual flow. Review every visible label that mentions rounds, sets, best rounds, or three attempts.
3. **#55 — Single-round tests:** depends on #53 and validates the adapted reducer and complete playthrough without replacing existing suites.
4. **#56 — Exit controls:** can be implemented after the baseline flow is single-round; add a reducer action and controls during play and at round end, using the existing shared button conventions.
5. **#57 — Exit tests:** depends on #56 and extends both reducer and playthrough coverage for a clean, safe return to the start screen.

## Key Design Decisions

- Make `ROUND_COUNT = 1` the sole format switch. Do not fork match state or create special single-round branches in components; existing `roundTimes.length`, shared count display, and reducer helpers should naturally support one recorded turn.
- Keep score helpers (`getPlayerBestRoundTime` / `getPlayerScoreMs`) backward-compatible because a single entry is trivially the player's score. Rename visible copy where needed without introducing unnecessary state or API churn.
- Treat exit as an explicit reducer action that returns `createInitialMatchState()`. This guarantees the current player, selected difficulties, timer state, pause state, and round records are all reset through one deterministic transition.
- In `PlayingScreen`, cancel any pending game-over delay on unmount and keep the existing hook cleanup behavior so exiting cannot dispatch a late `completeRound` action into a new match.
- Use the existing semantic buttons and `primary-button` interaction styles for exit confirmation. Make the action clear and available only in the requested playing and round-end states; avoid browser navigation or persistence.
- Update tests by changing the existing helpers and assertions instead of adding a parallel test harness. Cover direct player-one-to-handoff and player-two-to-results progression, accurate final winner selection, and exit reset behavior.
